SECTION 3 — CRITICAL ISSUE 2
PERMANENT DATABASE & NEWS DATA PERSISTENCE
==================================================

CURRENT PROBLEM:

News published through the live admin panel sometimes disappears after code updates and redeployment.

The project must be redesigned around a strict separation between application code and persistent application data.

CORE PRINCIPLE:

GITHUB STORES CODE.

SUPABASE STORES LIVE DATA.

NETLIFY HOSTS THE APPLICATION.

GOOGLE AI STUDIO MODIFIES THE CODE.

These systems must never be confused.

A. SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH

Supabase PostgreSQL must be the single source of truth for all dynamic website content.

This includes:

- News articles.
- News titles.
- News descriptions.
- News content.
- Featured images.
- Categories.
- Tags.
- Authors.
- Publication status.
- Publication dates.
- Breaking news.
- Featured news.
- Trending news.
- Admin-managed content.
- User-generated records where applicable.
- Existing website settings stored in the database.

All live content must be fetched from Supabase.

Do not hardcode published news into React components.

Do not store published news in localStorage as the primary database.

Do not store production news in static JSON files.

Do not create a second independent news database.

B. NEWS PUBLISHING WORKFLOW

When an administrator publishes news:

1. Authenticate the administrator.
2. Verify admin permissions.
3. Validate the news data.
4. Upload the image if necessary.
5. Obtain the confirmed image URL.
6. Insert or update the news record in Supabase PostgreSQL.
7. Verify the database response.
8. Return a success response only after the database operation succeeds.
9. Refresh or invalidate relevant frontend queries.
10. Update the live website.

The published news must remain available after:

- Browser refresh.
- Browser restart.
- Logout and login.
- Netlify redeployment.
- GitHub commit.
- GitHub branch update.
- Google AI Studio code modification.
- New application build.
- Frontend redesign.
- Application restart.

C. NEVER RESET PRODUCTION DATA

Inspect the existing codebase for dangerous operations such as:

- DROP TABLE.
- TRUNCATE TABLE.
- DELETE FROM without appropriate conditions.
- Database reset scripts.
- Destructive seed scripts.
- Automatic table recreation.
- Destructive migration logic.
- Startup scripts that overwrite production records.

Remove or repair unsafe operations.

Do not execute destructive SQL against the production database.

Do not run seed scripts automatically in production.

Do not recreate the database during deployment.

Do not overwrite live content with mock data.

Do not use hardcoded demo news as production content.

D. DATABASE MIGRATION SYSTEM

Implement a safe database migration strategy.

Requirements:

- Version-controlled SQL migrations.
- Idempotent migration scripts wherever practical.
- Non-destructive schema changes.
- Explicit migration ordering.
- Existing data preservation.
- Safe indexes.
- Safe constraints.
- Safe database changes.
- Clear migration documentation.

Never automatically drop production tables.

Never reset existing database records to match a new frontend version.

Database migrations must modify the schema without deleting existing news.

E. DATABASE INDEPENDENCE FROM FRONTEND DEPLOYMENT

This is extremely important.

A new frontend deployment must not recreate or reset the database.

The frontend must retrieve the latest data from Supabase at runtime.

For example:

Admin publishes news
       ↓
Supabase PostgreSQL
       ↓
Supabase Realtime / Database Query
       ↓
Live Website

A new GitHub commit must not interfere with this workflow.

A new Netlify deployment must not interfere with this workflow.

A new Google AI Studio session must not interfere with this workflow.

F. NEWS RECORD IDENTITY

Every news record must have a permanent unique identifier.

Use the existing primary key if correctly implemented.

Do not generate temporary IDs that change on every deployment.

Ensure that updates modify existing records rather than accidentally duplicating or replacing them.

Preserve:
- Created timestamps.
- Updated timestamps.
- Publication timestamps.
- Existing news IDs.
- Existing relationships.

G. BACKUP AND RECOVERY

Implement a documented database backup strategy.

Requirements:

- Explain Supabase backup options.
- Document how to export news data.
- Document how to restore database records.
- Protect existing data before major schema changes.
- Recommend regular backups.
- Never claim a backup exists unless it has been verified.

Do not store sensitive credentials inside GitHub.

